<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bảo Đại</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bao Dai, Nguyễn Phúc Vĩnh Thụy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bảo Đại was the last emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, reigning under French protection from 1926. He was made head of the Japanese-sponsored Empire of Vietnam in 1945 and abdicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> that August. The French returned him as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> in 1949, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Ngo Dinh Diem</a> deposed him in a referendum in 1955 and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bao-dai.jpg" alt="Bảo Đại" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_B%E1%BA%A3o_%C4%90%E1%BA%A1i_on_his_throne_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 March 1945 the Japanese overthrew French rule in a coup, interning French troops and officials, and encouraged <b>Bao Dai</b> in Vietnam, Norodom Sihanouk in Cambodia and Sisavang Vong in Laos to declare independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Khai Dinh (r. 1916–1925) cooperated closely with the French, and <b>Bao Dai</b>, emperor from 1926, attempted modest reforms after returning from education in France in 1932 but had little power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After a heavily rigged referendum deposed the former emperor <b>Bảo Đại</b>, Ngô Đình Diệm proclaimed the Republic of Vietnam in the south.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-26">26 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As France changed strategy to work with <b>Bao Dai</b>, the republic was renamed the Provisional Government of South Vietnam in 1947 and in 1948 merged into the Provisional Central Government of Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1950 <b>Bao Dai</b>, head of the State of Vietnam, decreed that the Central Highlands and other upland areas inhabited mainly by ethnic minorities would form the Hoang trieu Cuong tho, Crown Domain, directly under the Nguyen crown rather than the ordinary government.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-domain-of-the-nguyen-dynasty">Crown Domain of the Nguyen Dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>On 11 March Emperor <b>Bao Dai</b>, at Japan's urging, declared the treaties of protection with France void and proclaimed the independence of the Empire of Vietnam, covering Annam and Tonkin.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-vietnam">Empire of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
